{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean pushed head c17022cd40de12cb38fc8ea901c29316e9ef24ad. Architecture: affected layers are the application profile and pre-signing boundary in internal/app, the Workroom interpreter/fold and projections in internal/workroom, and their CLI/MCP/UI/documentation surfaces. The head changes the report lifecycle contract and documents it, but validateDirectReport does not preserve the same contract as fold.reportClaim. It scans every projected statement, does not filter decisions to effective, and uses the built-in StarterLifecycle rather than the active vocabulary already assembled by validateReportBasis. Consequently an ineffective promise can falsely block a valid direct report and an effective custom promise-lifecycle kind can be missed. The promised shape also checks the promise actor but does not reject request bases other than the unique governing request, so a report that the fold rejects can pass this boundary, be signed and appended, and spend depth. Security: these are integrity and authority-boundary failures at the pre-append gate; they create both false refusal and post-append ineffective durable acts from inputs whose mismatch is locally knowable. Request changes and mutation-sensitive application tests proving an ineffective promise does not block direct completion, a declared active promise kind does block it, and a promised report citing an unrelated request is refused before append with head and depth unchanged. Existing direct addressee and live built-in promise tests are necessary but not sufficient. Simplification: decide claim shape from one shared active-lifecycle/effective-record classifier, or expose one fold-derived claim summary, rather than reimplementing a partial second classifier with StarterLifecycle. This removes the drift without weakening any condition. The exact head, clean origin state, focused Go tests, UI tests, docs build, and unchanged refusal-before-append intent were otherwise checked; approval is withheld until the application boundary and fold agree on all admitted shapes.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:21c4cc3988bffb44988d4e0d50e2ba8ae4946287","head":"c17022cd40de12cb38fc8ea901c29316e9ef24ad","verdict":"changes-requested"}}